      "## Define the support record\n\nFor captive-portal access failure, capture approved location, network name, device class, operating system, browser, local time, portal URL as displayed, certificate warning state, connection state, user-visible error, and permitted screenshot reference. Label every item by source: customer statement, system event, approved documentation, onsite observation, or owner decision. A copied value without provenance can look precise while pointing to the wrong tenant, device, account, or time window. Minimize personal and security data; use durable references to protected systems instead of reproducing secrets in a general ticket.",
      "## Give a safe first response\n\nThe first response should ask the customer to stop if the portal presents a certificate or privacy warning, distinguish association from internet access, and use only the property or network owner’s published connection instructions. Tell the customer what is confirmed, what remains unknown, who owns the next decision, and when another update will arrive. A useful checkpoint reduces repeated effort without implying diagnosis or completion. If the customer describes immediate safety, active compromise, or another emergency trigger, follow the organization’s dedicated emergency or incident route rather than extending this ordinary workflow.",
