      "## Run the handoff in observable stages\n\nConfirm the intended network through an authoritative channel, remove assumptions about stored sessions, compare a second approved device only when permitted, document the portal transition, route warnings immediately, and retain a customer-safe fallback. At every transition, record the sending owner, receiving owner, acceptance event, evidence available at that moment, and condition for returning work. Assignment to a group or posting in chat is not proof that someone accepted responsibility. Until acceptance is visible, the current owner keeps the customer checkpoint and the safe interim state.",
      "## Anticipate the ways this case can mislead\n\nCommon failure paths include evil-twin network names, randomized device addresses, expired portal sessions, VPN interception, private DNS behavior, inaccessible consent controls, rate limiting, and advice copied from a different venue. Do not flatten these into a generic “user error” or “system issue.” Each alternative changes the evidence needed and the person who can decide. Preserve contradictions rather than selecting the most convenient explanation, and add later corrections without overwriting what the operator reasonably knew at the earlier checkpoint.",
      "## Use customer language that matches the evidence\n\nA bounded update could say: “The device is connected to a network but the portal has not established usable access. We are checking the approved network instructions; do not install a certificate or enter company credentials into an unexpected page.” Adapt names and times, but keep the distinction between observed state, action underway, and decision pending. Avoid unsupported restoration times, fault attribution, or promises about compensation. When an earlier update becomes wrong, issue a clear correction linked to the same case instead of silently editing the record.",
      "## Test before relying on the workflow\n\nUse synthetic accounts, harmless records, and approved test environments to replay a normal login, a misleading duplicate network, an expired session, keyboard-only navigation, a portal blocked by privacy settings, and a venue contact who cannot be reached. Observe the real frontline role rather than an administrator demonstration. A pass requires the expected stop, an accepted handoff, a usable alternate path where promised, and a record that another reviewer can reconstruct. Hidden facilitator help, private messages, or exceptional access should be recorded as failures of the operating design.",
      "## Measure outcomes, not ticket motion\n\nReview verified-network rate, portal completion evidence, unsafe-warning stops, fallback use, accessibility defects, accepted network-owner handoffs, and repeat contacts. Publish numerators, denominators, exclusions, and unresolved cases by consequence class. A quick reassignment can still be a failed handoff, and a controlled wait can be correct when the decision owner is gathering evidence. Read representative cases beside the aggregate so a small high-impact group is not concealed by a favorable average.",
      "## Close with durable evidence\n\nTo close captive-portal access failure, state whether access was restored, an alternate connection was used, the portal remained unverified, or the case moved to the customer security owner; preserve the exact limit of the finding. Closure means the customer-facing endpoint and the internal control state agree. A resolved label alone does not prove delivery, access, reversal, custody, or understanding. Keep residual work open under a named owner and reconcile future tickets, scheduled jobs, saved instructions, or dependent automations that could recreate the same failure.",
      "## Put the workflow into service\n\nBefore launch, publish the captive-portal access failure trigger, required record, permitted frontline actions, stopping conditions, decision owners, customer wording, update interval, after-hours route, evidence retention rule, and closure test in one controlled procedure. Give the ordinary support role a synthetic case and ask a second operator to continue from the handoff without private explanation. Record every missing field, unavailable permission, ambiguous instruction, inaccessible step, and owner-capacity gap. Correct those mechanisms, repeat the exercise with different evidence, and launch only the portion that the buyer can observe, govern, and reverse.",
