      "## Anticipate the ways this case can mislead\n\nCommon failure paths include similarly named printers, abandoned output, duplicate retries, badge mapping changes, offline spoolers, job expiry, wrong paper or finishing settings, shared accounts, and a dashboard state that lags the device. Do not flatten these into a generic “user error” or “system issue.” Each alternative changes the evidence needed and the person who can decide. Preserve contradictions rather than selecting the most convenient explanation, and add later corrections without overwriting what the operator reasonably knew at the earlier checkpoint.",
      "## Use customer language that matches the evidence\n\nA bounded update could say: “The job is recorded as held and has not been treated as printed or destroyed. Please avoid resubmitting it while the print owner checks the queue and device custody.” Adapt names and times, but keep the distinction between observed state, action underway, and decision pending. Avoid unsupported restoration times, fault attribution, or promises about compensation. When an earlier update becomes wrong, issue a clear correction linked to the same case instead of silently editing the record.",
      "## Test before relying on the workflow\n\nUse synthetic accounts, harmless records, and approved test environments to use harmless sample jobs for release, cancellation, expiry, offline recovery, badge mismatch, duplicate submission, and a job accidentally routed to another floor. Observe the real frontline role rather than an administrator demonstration. A pass requires the expected stop, an accepted handoff, a usable alternate path where promised, and a record that another reviewer can reconstruct. Hidden facilitator help, private messages, or exceptional access should be recorded as failures of the operating design.",
      "## Measure outcomes, not ticket motion\n\nReview jobs with attributable custody, duplicate-prevention success, time to accepted print-owner action, cancellation evidence, abandoned-output events, and verified customer closure. Publish numerators, denominators, exclusions, and unresolved cases by consequence class. A quick reassignment can still be a failed handoff, and a controlled wait can be correct when the decision owner is gathering evidence. Read representative cases beside the aggregate so a small high-impact group is not concealed by a favorable average.",
